+# The Salt Crown's metal: a PAIR of matte pewters. METALLIC is the number that
+# matters here, not roughness, and getting that backwards is why this design has
+# been rejected three rounds running.
+#
+# The crown kept coming back with BOTH of two apparently opposite complaints at
+# once - "the hottest specular on the upper half of the figure" AND "a lumpy grey
+# smudge / invisible at 184". Three passes treated those as two problems and
+# tuned roughness to split the difference (0.26 -> 0.52 -> 0.86). They are one
+# problem. At metallic 1.0 there is NO diffuse term at all, so a metal in this rig
+# - five small area lights against a 0.03 world - is a mirror of a black room and
+# shows up ONLY where a highlight happens to land. Raising roughness does not make
+# such a surface matte, it makes it ABSENT: it spreads the one light response
+# thinner while adding nothing back, so the ring reads as a hot pinpoint on one
+# facet and near-felt-black over the other 95% of itself. That is exactly the
+# "pinned at the wrong end" note - there is a lot of headroom between near-black
+# and competing with the gem, and full metallic cannot reach any of it.
+#
+# So, following the wrought arm below, which hit this same trap and solved it:
+# both of these are deliberately PART DIELECTRIC. At metallic 0.40 about 60% of
+# the response is Lambertian off the albedo, which holds a steady mid value across
+# the whole surface no matter where the lights are, and the roughness can then
+# stay high enough (0.72-0.82) that the remaining metallic lobe is broad and dull
+# with no hot spot anywhere. Dull AND present, which is what "matte regalia" needs
+# and what roughness alone was never able to deliver.
+#
+# The two values are a VALUE pair, not a shine pair - same metal, same roughness
+# band, different albedo - because separation at icon size needs surface AREA. The
+# whole outer half of each blade and the ring's upper band take M_CROWN_LIT;
+# everything turned away takes M_CROWN. A 1 px bright arris does not survive
+# downscaling whatever it is made of, which is why three passes of edge rims were
+# invisible at 184.
+#
+# LEVEL. Fixing the absence overshot straight past the target and the metal came
+# back as the brightest value on the model - measured off the render, the blades
+# ran mean 0.330 / p95 0.701 against the beard's 0.270 / 0.572, and out-massed the
+# gem at the top of frame. That inverts the one hierarchy the whole palette is
+# built on, and it is the same error as before with the sign flipped: this design
+# has now failed at BOTH ends of the same axis.
+#
+# So the albedo comes down hard while metallic stays at 0.40. Keeping metallic is
+# what keeps the surface present at all; the albedo is what sets where it sits.
+# Targets, all from the panel and mutually consistent: clearly darker than the
+# beard, roughly midway between the robe and the beard, at or below the silver hem
+# trim, gem unambiguously the brightest pixel. Roughness moves into the 0.60-0.75
+# band and there is no coat on either.
+#
+# Effective diffuse: body ~0.09, facet ~0.16, against the beard at 0.25 and the
+# robe at 0.011. The metal is now meant to be found by EDGE AND SILHOUETTE, never
+# by brightness - which is why this change and the flat-facet change below are one
+# idea in two places. Drop the value without giving the blades a real facet and
+# they would simply go back to being invisible.
