+    #
+    # This ring used to be split into a light outer arc, a mid inner arc and a
+    # near-black underside. Every panel since has called the circlet "a stack of
+    # two or three concentric bands" or "machined concentric ribs", and that split
+    # is where the extra bands were coming from: three material zones stacked up a
+    # 16 mm tall torus draw three horizontal lines across the hat's cleanest zone,
+    # which is precisely the banding the design is supposed to avoid. Chasing it
+    # with geometry would have been chasing the wrong thing.
+    #
+    # There is nothing to replace it with, either. Now that the felt genuinely
+    # crushes and rolls over both edges, the ring is read from the cloth pinched
+    # around it - found by edge and silhouette, exactly as the note asks - so it
+    # does not need an internal value break of its own, and at this albedo it could
+    # not afford one anyway.
+    pass
+
+    # NOTE there is deliberately no hat gem any more. The old buckle stone, and
+    # then the same stone re-seated in this ring, put a second violet point at
+    # head height - and violet is the one colour the staff gem has to own
+    # outright. The circlet carries the eye on value alone.
+
+    back = Vector((0.0, 1.0, 0.0))
+    ck, sk = cos(CREST_RAKE), sin(CREST_RAKE)
+
+    def width_at(t):
+        """Blade half-width down the length, as a fraction of SPIRE_W.
+
+        A leaf, not a needle: near full width for the lower two thirds, then a
+        real point over the top third. Every earlier pass used a power law from
+        the root, which looks fine in section but spends most of the blade's
+        length under a pixel at icon size - and a blade that is sub-pixel over
+        80% of itself is a smudge, not a spike.
+        """
